Direct3D 9, being implemented.

The plan's Phase 3 is D3D9 on the pieces D3D11 already has: a D3D9 device over this build's D3D11 device, its chains
over DXGI's presenter, and draws through airconv, so a D3D9 game reaches Metal by the same road a D3D11 one does.

# Milestone 1 (done): the device and presentation
What a build with -Denable_d3d9=true makes (the option is off by default, so a contributor who does not ask for D3D9
gets the build they had):

  * Direct3DCreate9: the IDirect3D9 object over DXGI's adapters, which are this build's Metal devices. Adapter
    identity is DXGI's (the Metal device's name, vendor and device ids); the caps are the ones that adapter's D3D11
    device has, with the texture size of its feature level. The caps say what is there: a HAL that presents, and no
    shaders, fixed function pipeline, depth buffer or draw yet.
  * CreateDevice with D3DDEVTYPE_HAL for a window: a D3D11 device over the same adapter, and the window's implicit
    swap chain through DXGI's factory (a flip model DISCARD chain, at least two buffers).
  * Clear of the back buffer, and Present: what was cleared is shown, four frames in a row
    (tests/d3d9/d3d9_present.cpp checks the fourth frame as it checks the first).
  * A surface read back: D3DPOOL_SYSTEMMEM surfaces are staging textures, and GetRenderTargetData copies the back
    buffer into one, which LockRect then reads.
  * The back buffer a chain hands out stays the same buffer across a Present. The chain turns its buffers over by
    giving buffer 0 the next buffer's storage (DXGI's flip model as this build implements it), so the view and the
    surface taken from it stay the frame's, and a game that keeps its surface draws into the frame being drawn.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_present.cpp is the test of this milestone, a window test since it presents to one. It reads each
frame back twice: out of the back buffer through GetRenderTargetData before Present, and out of the window through
GDI after it (the frame reaches the window through GDI because the test asks for dxgi.presentThroughGDI, since
nothing reads a Metal view's pixels back).

# Milestone 2 (done): geometry through the fixed-function path
What a build of this milestone draws, over the D3D11 device and draw path D3D11 already has:

  * IDirect3DVertexBuffer9 and IDirect3DIndexBuffer9: CreateVertexBuffer and CreateIndexBuffer (D3DPOOL_DEFAULT or
    D3DPOOL_MANAGED, D3DFMT_INDEX16/INDEX32), Lock and Unlock with D3D9's rules (one lock at a time; DISCARD and
    NOOVERWRITE only on a dynamic buffer and never both; a lock's range must fit the buffer), and GetDesc. The D3D11
    buffer under each is dynamic, which the D3D11 core only maps for a whole-buffer discard, so the wrapper keeps the
    buffer's bytes and Unlock uploads them: a lock that does not discard keeps what was there, as D3D9's does.
  * SetFVF with D3DFVF_XYZRHW | D3DFVF_DIFFUSE, a vertex already in screen space with a colour. Any other format is
    D3DERR_INVALIDCALL and the device keeps the format it had (tests/d3d9/d3d9_triangle.cpp checks that with
    D3DFVF_XYZ, which needs the world transform this milestone does not do).
  * SetStreamSource (stream 0, an offset and a stride) and SetStreamSourceFreq (1, no instancing), SetIndices.
  * DrawPrimitive, DrawIndexedPrimitive and DrawPrimitiveUP, all three through shaders of the library's own (a vertex
    shader that maps the screen-space vertex through the viewport constant, and a pixel shader that writes the vertex
    colour) under the render state milestone 3 brings, which starts as D3DRENDERSTATETYPE's documented defaults: fill
    solid, cull counter-clockwise (so a screen-clockwise triangle is a front face and is drawn), depth test off until
    the device has an automatic depth-stencil surface or an application asks for it, source blend ONE and destination
    blend ZERO. The tests' own screen-space triangles are wound counter-clockwise, so those tests turn culling off.
  * What a draw refuses, with D3D9's code: a draw with no stream source, an indexed draw whose index range or vertex
    range does not fit the buffers it names, a base vertex that puts the range outside them, an FVF this milestone
    cannot transform, and a vertex buffer whose stride is not the format's. A draw of no primitives is S_OK and draws
    nothing.
  * The device holds the vertex buffer it is the stream source of: an application's Release does not take it away,
    and the draw after the release still draws.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_triangle.cpp draws four frames in a row, each a triangle of a known colour at a known place (a
vertex buffer, an index buffer, vertices in memory, and a vertex buffer the application released), and reads each
one back out of the back buffer and out of the window after Present. tests/d3d9/d3d9_draw_contract.cpp is what the
draw path refuses and what it keeps between calls.

# Milestone 3 (done): render states, depth-stencil surfaces and render targets
What a frame is drawn with and into, so that a basic frame is correct rather than merely visible:

  * SetRenderState and GetRenderState for the states the fixed-function path can honour: D3DRS_ZENABLE,
    ZWRITEENABLE, ZFUNC, CULLMODE, FILLMODE (D3DFILL_SOLID and D3DFILL_WIREFRAME; D3DFILL_POINT has no D3D11 fill
    mode and is refused as a value), SHADEMODE (D3DSHADE_GOURAUD and D3DSHADE_FLAT, which is its own pixel shader with
    a nointerpolation colour; D3DSHADE_PHONG is refused), D3DRS_ALPHABLENDENABLE, SRCBLEND, DESTBLEND, BLENDOP and
    COLORWRITEENABLE. A state this build has no meaning for (lighting, fog, alpha test, stencil state, scissor) is
    D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, said once, and a value that cannot be honoured is refused the same way; either leaves the
    device with the value it had, so a frame is never drawn under a state nobody asked for. The values a device
    starts with are the ones D3DRENDERSTATETYPE documents: CULLMODE D3DCULL_CCW, SRCBLEND D3DBLEND_ONE, DESTBLEND
    D3DBLEND_ZERO, ZWRITEENABLE TRUE, ZFUNC D3DCMP_LESSEQUAL, FILLMODE D3DFILL_SOLID, SHADEMODE D3DSHADE_GOURAUD,
    ALPHABLENDENABLE FALSE, BLENDOP D3DBLENDOP_ADD, COLORWRITEENABLE all four channels, TEXTUREFACTOR 0xFFFFFFFF, and
    ZENABLE TRUE only for a device whose chain made an automatic depth-stencil surface (FALSE otherwise).
  * CreateDepthStencilSurface (D3DFMT_D16, D24X8, D24X4S4, D24S8 and D32, D3DPOOL_DEFAULT), SetDepthStencilSurface and
    GetDepthStencilSurface. The surface has to be the target's size, and a target of another size than the depth
    surface is refused rather than bound half a pair. EnableAutoDepthStencil in the present parameters makes one of
    AutoDepthStencilFormat at device creation, so a game that asked for depth has it from its first frame.
  * Clear with D3DCLEAR_TARGET, D3DCLEAR_ZBUFFER and D3DCLEAR_STENCIL: the colour of the target being drawn into, and
    the depth and stencil of the surface set. A colour clear leaves depth as it was, and only D3DCLEAR_ZBUFFER resets it.
    The stencil is cleared with the depth, though no draw this milestone makes tests it yet. A clear of rects clears the
    colour of each of them (clamped to the target, and a rect that names no pixel clears none), and leaves depth and
    stencil as they were, with the library saying so once: this backend's clear of part of a depth-stencil view is not
    rect-accurate yet.
  * ValidateDevice answers one pass for state it can draw with, and GetAvailableTextureMem answers what the Metal
    device recommends it may still take (IDXGIAdapter3::QueryVideoMemoryInfo: budget less what its textures have
    taken), which is the figure a title reads to size its textures.
  * CreateRenderTarget, SetRenderTarget (target 0; a target index but 0, a null surface and a depth surface are
    refused) and GetRenderTarget: Clear and the draws go to the target set, and the back buffer keeps what it had until
    SetRenderTarget names it again. A target brings its own viewport: setting one places the viewport on the whole of
    it (Wine's device_reset_viewport_state does the same), so a game that switches targets and draws without another
    SetViewport draws over the new target rather than over the old one's place in it. Present still presents the
    chain's back buffer, as D3D9's does.
  * SetViewport and GetViewport: the viewport places the vertex map (its origin and size) and is the draw's clip
    rectangle (D3D11's scissor), so a pixel outside it is not drawn whatever the triangle covers; a viewport of no size,
    outside the target, or with its depths outside 0 to 1 is refused and the device keeps the one it had.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_states.cpp is the state test: the defaults a device starts with, two triangles at different depths in
both drawing orders, depth off, depth writes off, ZFUNC, a colour clear against a depth clear, both cull windings with
both cull modes, wireframe against solid, blending under the default ONE/ZERO factors and SRCALPHA/INVSRCALPHA and
ONE/ONE, the colour write mask, GOURAUD against FLAT, and the states and values that are refused. It reports every state on its own: each SetRenderState of a state under test and each
pixel it is read out of is a check, and a state the device refuses is a `wrong:` line the run carries on past, so the
same test against milestone 2's device (which has no render state at all) named 36 wrongs across depth, cull, fill,
blend, colour write and shade instead of stopping at the first missing call. tests/d3d9/d3d9_targets.cpp is the
surfaces and viewport test: a custom target that takes the draw while the back buffer does not, the viewport it brings
and the one a draw is placed and clipped by, the depth surface on it, the automatic depth-stencil surface, and the
targets, depths and viewports that are refused.

The device's source is split by what a frame needs: d3d9_device.cpp has the object, its chain, its back buffer and
the surfaces a frame is read back into, d3d9_device_targets.cpp the render states, depth-stencil surfaces, render
targets and viewport, and d3d9_device_draw.cpp the buffers and draws, over the class in d3d9_device.hpp.

# Milestone 4a (done): textures and the texture stage
What a draw samples, and the one stage that samples it (this build's caps say MaxTextureBlendStages and
MaxSimultaneousTextures are 1, so a second stage or sampler is refused rather than kept by nobody):

  * CreateTexture for D3DPOOL_MANAGED and D3DPOOL_DEFAULT textures of the formats d3d9_common.hpp names
    (D3DFMT_A8R8G8B8 and X8R8G8B8 and the handful around them), with the levels asked for or (none asked for) the whole
    chain down to one texel, and GetLevelCount, GetLevelDesc and GetSurfaceLevel. A managed texture's level 0 is the CPU's: LockRect writes it and
    UnlockRect uploads it and makes the levels below it out of it (their own GenerateMipSubLevels makes them again).
    A level of a texture is a surface of the device, and a lock of that level through the texture or through the
    surface is the same lock (IMTLD3D9LockTarget). A default-pool texture is made with the one level nothing in this
    build could fill yet, said once.
  * A texture's level desc is the texture's: its format, its pool (a managed texture's level is managed, a target's is
    default, a readback surface is system memory) and the size the chain halves down to.
  * SetTexture/GetTexture (stage 0, and the device holds what was set), SetSamplerState/GetSamplerState and
    SetTextureStageState/GetTextureStageState.
  * The stage's operations, as the textured pixel shaders' constants: D3DTOP_DISABLE, SELECTARG1, SELECTARG2, MODULATE,
    ADD and SUBTRACT for the colour op and the alpha op apart, over the arguments D3DTA_TEXTURE, D3DTA_DIFFUSE,
    D3DTA_CURRENT (the vertex colour at stage 0, which is the only stage there is) and D3DTA_TFACTOR, whose colour is
    D3DRS_TEXTUREFACTOR. An argument with a modifier (a complement or an alpha replication) is another argument and is
    refused, as is an operation, an argument or a texture-coordinate setting this build does not carry.
  * The sampler: the three filters (each pick is taken on its own whatever the other two are, and the draw samples
    through the closest D3D11 filter to the three -- see d3d9_texture.cpp), the address modes WRAP, MIRROR, CLAMP and
    MIRRORONCE for each of u, v and w, and MAXANISOTROPY from 1 to 16. A border address mode, a level-of-detail bias, a
    maximum mip level, a border colour, an sRGB texture and an element index are refused instead of sampled as
    something else (the states whose only usable value is this build's own -- a bias of none, the top level, a border
    nobody reads -- are taken).
  * What D3D9 says about a stage with no texture: a stage whose first colour argument is D3DTA_TEXTURE is not processed
    at all while no texture is bound, which for stage 0 is the vertex colour the frame already carries, and a texture
    argument in any other place samples a white texel (1, 1, 1, 1).
  * What a texture call refuses, with D3D9's code: a usage flag (dynamic, render target, automatic mipmaps), a
    system-memory or scratch pool, a level count past the chain, a format this build has none of, a texture of no
    pixels, a fourth level of a size that has three, a lock of a texture whose bytes are not the CPU's, a second lock of
    the same texture, an unlock of a level that is not the locked one, and a stage or sampler the device has not.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_texture.cpp is the test of this milestone: an 8 by 8 texture written through its top level, whose four
quadrants are four texels of different colours (a pixel says which part of the texture it sampled) and whose last texel
is a colour of its own (so a pixel says the rows and columns arrived whole). It reads the frame back out of the back
buffer and, after Present, out of the window: the texels where their coordinates say, the vertex colour multiplying
and adding to them (MODULATE and ADD), each operation and argument, the texture's own alpha against the vertex's, a
wrapping sampler repeating the texture against a clamping one holding its edge, the anisotropic filter sampling the
same texels, and SHADEMODE (a FLAT draw takes one vertex's colour for the whole triangle, a GOURAUD one interpolates;
the test's triangle carries three colours so the two are not the same frame).

# Milestone 4b: the rest of what a game draws with
The rest of the fixed-function pipeline and the resources around it, still over D3D11: vertex declarations and state
blocks, the world/view/projection transform for D3DFVF_XYZ vertices, lighting and the texture-coordinate formats, the
remaining texture features a game asks for (a second and further stages, D3DUSAGE_DYNAMIC and D3DUSAGE_RENDERTARGET
textures, cube and volume textures, D3DXFill-style level writing), the rest of the render states (lighting, fog, alpha
test, stencil operations, point fill, scissor), DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP, and queries.

# Milestone 5: the pipeline a game's shaders run on
D3D9 vertex and pixel shaders through airconv (SM 1.x to 3.0 are D3D9's, and airconv takes DXBC), constants and
sampler state, the rest of the draw calls (DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP), queries and occlusion.

# What is not there yet, and is refused rather than pretended
  * anything that makes a resource or shader this build does not have (cube and volume textures, scratch-pool or
    dynamic textures, vertex shaders and pixel shaders): E_NOTIMPL or D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, with the library saying so
    once. `SetVertexShader(NULL)` and `SetPixelShader(NULL)` answer S_OK, as D3D9 does: they choose the fixed-function
    pipeline, which is the only one this milestone has
  * a vertex format other than D3DFVF_XYZRHW | D3DFVF_DIFFUSE, with or without one set of texture coordinates:
    D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, and the device keeps the format it had, so a frame is not drawn with a transform nobody asked
    for
  * DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP: D3DERR_INVALIDCALL (nothing is emitted, so nothing is silently missing from a frame)
  * a render state this milestone has no meaning for, and a value it cannot honour (D3DFILL_POINT, D3DSHADE_PHONG,
    BOTHSRCALPHA, BOTHINVSRCALPHA and the D3DRS_BLENDFACTOR factors): D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, with the device keeping what it had
  * SetLOD beyond the top level: the call answers with the level the texture is on (D3D9's SetLOD answers with the one
    it had) and says once, in the library's log, that the most detailed level is the top one -- the levels below it are
    made out of it, so a texture is sampled from level 0 whatever it asks for. GetAutoGenFilterType answers D3DTEXF_LINEAR
    and SetAutoGenFilterType is D3DERR_INVALIDCALL: the automatic mip filter is a D3DUSAGE_AUTOGENMIPMAP texture's, and
    this build makes a managed texture's levels with D3D11's own generator
  * a render target or a depth-stencil surface with more than one sample, and a render target the CPU locks:
    D3DERR_NOTAVAILABLE or D3DERR_INVALIDCALL rather than a target that is not what it says
  * clearing the depth or stencil of part of a surface (the colour of its rects is cleared, and its depth and stencil
    are left as they were, said once)
  * the second and further texture stages and samplers, lights, vertex declarations, state blocks, queries:
    D3DERR_INVALIDCALL
  * Reset, fullscreen chains, additional swap chains, GetFrontBufferData, CreateAdditionalSwapChain: not implemented

# Seeing what a game asked for
Every call this layer refuses says so once, in the library's log, with what it was asked for: the format, the shader
version, the state, and the call itself. A game that stops at one of them names what to implement next instead of
leaving its HRESULT to guess, which is how this layer is grown against real titles.

A build writes that log where `MULLION_LOG_PATH` names a directory, as `<the game's executable>_mullion.log`; without
it the lines go to Wine's console. `MULLION_LOG_LEVEL` chooses how much is kept ("info" by default, "warn" for the
refusals alone). Both are the environment's, so a bottle's environment is where they are set for a title.
